  "solution": "<p><strong>1.</strong> The cubic factors as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the crossings in the stated interval are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The graph is odd; its two halves have equal geometric area even though their signed integrals cancel.</p><p><strong>2.</strong> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the curve is below the axis, and on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> it is above. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</p><p><strong>3.</strong> The required total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mn>2</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0222B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>d</mi><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>25</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>41</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Answer E. Adding the absolute values of all four interval integrals gives the same total.</p>"
